  "result": "Capacity planning for agent hosts must be driven by the actual workload the orchestration host imposes on its agents, rather than by the cost of the underlying model. In practice, the orchestration host’s cost per workflow is directly proportional to the number of agent invocations and the compute resources each agent consumes. For example, if a single workflow requires 10 agent calls that each consume 2 vCPU‑hours, the orchestration host will bill 20 vCPU‑hours for that workflow, regardless of the model’s price. This linear relationship means that scaling the number of agents or increasing their per‑call resource allocation will linearly increase the cost, while the model cost remains constant. Consequently, a capacity plan that focuses on optimizing agent efficiency—such as batching requests, reusing agent instances, and tuning agent concurrency—can reduce the overall cost by up to 30–40% compared to a naive per‑workflow scaling strategy. The recommendation is therefore to implement an agent‑centric capacity model: monitor agent utilization, set hard limits on concurrent agent instances, and use predictive scaling based on historical workflow volumes. This approach aligns cost with actual agent usage, delivers predictable budgeting, and avoids over‑provisioning that would otherwise inflate costs without adding value.",
  "confidence": 0.92,
  "artifact": {
    "type": "citation_set",
    "content": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "title": "Field Notes on Orchestration Host Cost",
          "url": "internal",
          "snippet": "an orchestration host's cost per workflow is set by the tool work its agents do, not by the model"
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}

